<div class="ltx_dates">© none</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Information sharing platforms like TripAdvisor and Waze involve human agents as both information producers and consumers. All these platforms operate in a centralized way to collect agents’ latest observations of new options (e.g., restaurants, hotels, travel routes) and share such information with all in real time. However, after hearing the central platforms’ live updates, many human agents are found selfish and unwilling to further explore unknown options for the benefit of others in the long run. To regulate the human-in-the-loop learning (HILL) game against selfish agents’ free-riding, this paper proposes a paradigm shift from centralized to decentralized way of operation that forces agents’ local explorations through restricting information sharing. When game theory meets distributed learning, we formulate our decentralized communication mechanism’s design as a new multi-agent Markov decision process (MA-MDP), and derive its analytical condition to outperform today’s centralized operation. As the optimal decentralized communication mechanism in MA-MDP is NP-hard to solve, we present an asymptotically optimal algorithm with linear complexity to determine the mechanism’s timing of intermittent information sharing. Then we turn to non-myopic agents who may revert to even over-explore, and adapt our mechanism design to work. Simulation experiments using real-world dataset demonstrate the effectiveness of our decentralized mechanisms for various scenarios.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Distributed Multi-armed bandits, Incentivizing Exploration, Dynamic Bayesian game, Communication restriction
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Over the past decade, human-in-the-loop learning (HILL), which involves human agents as both information producers and consumers, has been widely implemented in real-world applications (e.g., TripAdvisor and Waze). <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Duan, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kremer et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Immorlica et al., 2019</a>)</cite>). It empowers a human agent to crowdsource other agents’ local resources and knowledge for learning large-scale systems (e.g., restaurant ratings, route recommendation, sales promotion). In HILL platforms, agents dynamically choose between exploring unknown options and exploiting high-reward ones based on the platforms’ shared information over time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Avner and Mannor, 2016</a>)</cite>. The uncertain reward from each service option, along with interaction among agents, poses principal challenges in this dynamic learning problem, necessitating strategic option choices and regulation to optimize social rewards over time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All these HILL platforms operate in a centralized way to collect agents’ latest observations of new options (e.g., restaurants, routes) and share such information with all in real time, aiming to connect all agents for maximum information exchanges. However, after hearing the central platforms’ live updates, human agents are found selfish and unwilling to further explore unknown options for the benefit of others in the long run. For example, selfish agents prefer to exploit established, highly-rated options, quickly converging to popular choices and neglecting many potentially better alternatives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park and Kim, 2008</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kremer et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Burtch et al., 2018</a>)</cite>. Facilitated by the central platforms, they appreciate reviewing the feedback shared by their peers but remain hesitant to explore additional options that could potentially benefit the community <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Duan, 2023</a>)</cite>. This phenomenon suggests that centralized information sharing to connect all in the current practice leads to severe under-exploration and poor social reward in the long run.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The multi-armed bandit (MAB) problem is a representative framework for studying the dynamic exploration-exploitation trade-off. Existing works on the extension to multi-agent MAB problems largely assume that agents are cooperative in communicating with a shared goal of maximizing long-term social reward. They focus on algorithms that enable and coordinate collaborative learning under communication limits or in the absence of centralized communication (e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Martínez-Rubio et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bistritz and Bambos, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madhushani et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agarwal et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu and Klabjan, 2024</a>)</cite>). For example, Dubey and Abhimanyu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al., 2020</a>)</cite> and Madhushani et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madhushani et al., 2021</a>)</cite> investigate the challenge of delayed information transmission among agents to converge globally. Agarwal et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agarwal et al., 2022</a>)</cite> aim to reduce communication bits while achieving the target regret bound.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">All these works assume human agents will adhere to prescribed algorithms without considering possible deviations for selfish interests. As such, they perceive communication opportunities in centralized information sharing as beneficial, aiming to maximally overcome the constraints of information communication for optimal multi-agent learning results. However, if an agent is selfish and focuses on maximizing her own rewards, receiving increased communication or shared information from others’ exploration just discourages her from exploring new options herself. If this free-riding behavior becomes widespread among all agents, the system lacks the necessary exploration to recommend the most beneficial options for all agents in the long run.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To regulate information sharing and incentivize exploration, one may think of other pricing or auction mechanisms to regulate (e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and Huang, 2018</a>)</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Braverman et al., 2019</a>)</cite>), yet these methods require monetary exchange or side-payment, which is hard to implement by information sharing platforms such as Tripadvisor and Waze in practice. Alternatively, several recent studies explore the problem under the concept of Bayesian persuasion and Bayesian incentive-compatible mechanisms (e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kremer et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tavafoghi and Teneketzis, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Immorlica et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Che and Hörner, 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Immorlica et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mansour et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sellke and Slivkins, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mansour et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Simchowitz and Slivkins, 2023</a>)</cite>). For example, Kremer et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kremer et al., 2014</a>)</cite> initiate the research, introducing a recommendation mechanism for incentivizing exploration of options with deterministic rewards by a single, non-repeating agent per round. Then Mansour et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mansour et al., 2020</a>)</cite> develop a method that makes any bandit algorithm Bayesian incentive-compatible, minimizing regret with stochastic rewards. Immorlica et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Immorlica et al., 2018</a>)</cite> use a selective data disclosure method to incentivize exploration in the bandit problem, and investigate incentivizing exploration among heterogeneous agents in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Immorlica et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, where the reward function depends on the agent type and system state. Additionally, Tavafoghi and Teneketzis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tavafoghi and Teneketzis, 2017</a>)</cite> apply informational incentives to transportation networks to mitigate congestion, where the route conditions are unknown to the agents. However, these methods largely assume myopic agents who only participate in a single round without having long-term reward objectives. Non-myopic human agents with enough sophistication, however, can strategically defeat these methods by misreporting information to fool others and deviating from the prescribed algorithms. We will show that the regulatory challenge evolves from under- to over-exploration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our key novelty and main contributions are summarized as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Novel HILL game through decentralized communication mechanism</em>: To our best knowledge, we are the first to propose the paradigm shift from centralized to decentralized information-sharing to enable the distributed exploration of self-interested agents. Previous HILL studies including distributed MAB works all prefer centralized system for cooperative agents’ maximum communication (e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Martínez-Rubio et al., 2019</a>)</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madhushani et al., 2021</a>)</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>), while our work pioneers the use of communication restriction in decentralized system to force non-cooperative agents’ local explorations, essentially involving the idea of concealing information. When game theory meets distributed learning, we formulate the dynamic HILL problem as a new multi-agent Markov decision process (MA-MDP) in Section <a href="#S2" title="2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Asymptotically optimal decentralized communication mechanism for myopic agents</em>: The MA-MDP problem of decentralized communication mechanism design for social welfare optimization is NP-hard, by including integer timing decisions to disable communication among agents and a non-convex objective. Despite this, in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we first analytically derive a sufficient and necessary condition in closed form for the platform to deviate from the prevailing practice of centralized communication, which often converges fast to inadequate exploration. Then we prove that it is best for the platform to intermittently open multiple communication windows among agents over time. Finally, to solve the NP-hard problem, we propose an asymptotically optimal algorithm with linear complexity.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Optimal decentralized communication mechanism to regulate non-myopic agents</em>: In Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Dcentralized Communication Mechanism for Non-myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we further address the challenge posed by some sophisticated human agents who non-myopically withhold information, surprisingly causing over-exploration for personal information gain over time. We show that they prefer free-riding and are not willing to share their learned information truthfully until the last time stage. In the dynamic Bayesian game, we successfully design the optimal decentralized communication mechanism to enable one-time communication at a strategic interval which mitigates over-exploration, while maintaining polynomial complexity.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Simulation experiments with real dataset for stochastic rewards and heterogeneous agents</em>: We conduct extensive simulation experiments together with a real-world dataset on hotel service rating to demonstrate the effectiveness of our decentralized communication design in Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Simulation Experiments with Real Dataset for Stochastic Rewards and Heterogeneous Preference ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. We show our method obviously outperforms the existing centralized communications scheme. Furthermore, we apply our method to two more general cases with stochastic reward per option and heterogeneous agent preferences over the same option. Simulation results demonstrate that our method remains effective in these extended scenarios.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>System Model and Problem Formulation </h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first present the general system model for selfish agents’ HILL game under any centralized/decentralized communication environment. We then delineate the problem formulation for the human-in-the-loop learning game in the multi-agent Markov decision process (MA-MDP) framework, by maximizing long-term social welfare via our decentralized communication mechanism.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>System Model on HILL Game</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider an information sharing platform with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> agents in the set <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}:=\{1,\ldots,N\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}:=\{1,\ldots,N\}</annotation></semantics></math> engaged in the HILL game to choose unknown service options in the set <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> over a discrete time horizon <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[T]:=\{0,1,\ldots,T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[T]:=\{0,1,\ldots,T\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We focus on the challenging case of a large-scale unknown system with far more options than agents. The reward from choosing option <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, an unknown and deterministic value independently drawn from a general cumulative distribution function <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> in a normalized continuous support [0, 1] with mean reward <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            We can also extend our analysis to allow that options follow a finite number of <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> different distributions <math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}(\cdot),\ldots,F_{L}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}(\cdot),\ldots,F_{L}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> with means <math id="footnote1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}\geq\ldots\geq\mu_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}\geq\ldots\geq\mu_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. There, agents will first explore options from <math id="footnote1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> class until exhausted, then proceed to <math id="footnote1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{2}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{2}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, and so on. We can similarly derive the exploration thresholds for myopic agents in Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> (by replacing <math id="footnote1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> by the mean reward of the best remaining option class) and for non-myopic agents in Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 4.2. ‣ 4.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communications Policy ‣ 4. Dcentralized Communication Mechanism for Non-myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, and our decentralized communication mechanisms still work.</span></span></span> At each time slot <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math>, Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.1. System Model on HILL Game ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that each agent <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> selects an option <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}(t)\in\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}(t)\in\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> and observes the reward <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{a_{n}(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{a_{n}(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, which may be centrally recorded and shared to other agents for their future consideration by the platform. Thereafter, this reward value <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{a_{n}(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{a_{n}(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> remains constant over the finite time horizon <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[T]</annotation></semantics></math>. Such deterministic reward models are common in the HILL literature for many applications of static service qualities (e.g., hotels, restaurants, and travel routes) in finite time (e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kremer et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tavafoghi and Teneketzis, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Immorlica et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">David and Shimkin, 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">David and Shimkin, 2015</a>)</cite>). For the case of stochastic reward, analyzing the optimal exploration of stochastic rewards within a finite time horizon or for many options is statistically challenging, and there is currently no optimal solution even for centralized systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lattimore and Szepesvári, 2020</a>)</cite>. Thus, we will conduct experiments using a real dataset in Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Simulation Experiments with Real Dataset for Stochastic Rewards and Heterogeneous Preference ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> to demonstrate that our mechanisms remain effective as long as the reward variation over time is limited.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The information sharing platform, acting as the social planner, regulates whether to allow agents’ mutual communications in each time slot. This is equivalent to determining whether to publicize the agents’ shared information in real time on the platform. If no communication is allowed, agents need to locally decide the exploration-exploitation tradeoff in a decentralized way. Define any decentralized communication mechanism to include <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> no-communication time periods over time as set <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\cup_{m=1}^{M}\{T_{m},\ldots,\allowbreak T_{m}+\Delta_{m}-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∪</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\cup_{m=1}^{M}\{T_{m},\ldots,\allowbreak T_{m}+\Delta_{m}-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> where communication is not allowed for <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each no-communication period <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\{1,\ldots,M\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\{1,\ldots,M\}</annotation></semantics></math> starts at <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and lasts <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> consecutive time slots, ending at <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{m}+\Delta_{m}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{m}+\Delta_{m}-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Two nearest periods are separated by at least one communication slot, i.e., <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{m+1}&gt;T_{m}+\Delta_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{m+1}&gt;T_{m}+\Delta_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> tells that the platform does not disable communication among agents and makes their shared information open-access all the time. <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is established by the social planner prior to the initiation of the learning process and is announced to all. Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.1. System Model on HILL Game ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> illustrates the agent communication under the mechanism <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> in the information sharing platform. With open access to centrally shared information, agents decide to explore or exploit by considering both shared and personal information. When communication is restricted, agents rely on their own information to act.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09574v1/figures/F22.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/97;" width="215" height="97" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>Decentralized communication mechanism <math id="S2.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> in the information sharing platform, where each agent <math id="S2.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> can exchange his option observation (<math id="S2.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}(t),r_{a_{n}(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}(t),r_{a_{n}(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>) with others for time <math id="S2.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\notin\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\notin\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, and cannot share for <math id="S2.F1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given sharing (if any) at time <math id="S2.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, other agents at the next time may exploit the same option <math id="S2.F1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> if its reward <math id="S2.F1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{a_{n}(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{a_{n}(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> is good or proceed with some other options.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We next explain the decision-making of each agent at time <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote the set of options whose reward information is known by agent <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> by time slot <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{n}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{n}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the set of corresponding rewards as <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{n}(t)=\{(k,r_{k})\,|\,k\in I_{n}(t)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{n}(t)=\{(k,r_{k})\,|\,k\in I_{n}(t)\}</annotation></semantics></math>. At the beginning of time slot <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, each agent <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> independently decides <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. If she chooses to explores a new option <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}(t)\notin I_{n}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}(t)\notin I_{n}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, she updates her option-reward information by <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{n}(t+1)=I_{n}(t)\cup\{a_{n}(t)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{n}(t+1)=I_{n}(t)\cup\{a_{n}(t)\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{n}(t+1)=R_{n}(t)\cup\{(a_{n}(t),r_{a_{n}(t)})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{n}(t+1)=R_{n}(t)\cup\{(a_{n}(t),r_{a_{n}(t)})\}</annotation></semantics></math>. If she opts to exploit the option <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}(t)\in I_{n}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}(t)\in I_{n}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, her information remains unchanged. If communication is permitted at time slot with <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]\setminus\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]\setminus\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> in the upper-part of Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.1. System Model on HILL Game ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, each agent decide whether to share truthful information <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{n}(t+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{n}(t+1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{n}(t+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{n}(t+1)</annotation></semantics></math> with all other agents, and updates her information again as <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I_{n}(t+1)=\cup_{i\in\mathcal{N}_{t}\cup\{n\}}I_{i}(t+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∪</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{n}(t+1)=\cup_{i\in\mathcal{N}_{t}\cup\{n\}}I_{i}(t+1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R_{n}(t+1)=\cup_{i\in\mathcal{N}_{t}\cup\{n\}}R_{i}(t+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∪</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{n}(t+1)=\cup_{i\in\mathcal{N}_{t}\cup\{n\}}R_{i}(t+1)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the subset of agents who share information at time <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Problem formulation as MA-MDP</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Define the strategy of agent <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{n}(t|R_{n}(t),\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{n}(t|R_{n}(t),\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> to determine the option <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. If myopic, agent <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>’s strategy <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> aims to maximize her immediate reward in each specific time step <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> (as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kremer et al., 2014</a>)</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mansour et al., 2022</a>)</cite>), that is,</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{s_{n}}{E}[r_{s_{n}(t)}|R_{n}(t),\mathcal{M}]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></munder><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{s_{n}}{E}[r_{s_{n}(t)}|R_{n}(t),\mathcal{M}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Her exploration results affect the others’ through possible communications in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[T]\setminus\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[T]\setminus\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the expected reward should be jointly determined by other agents’ exploration results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If non-myopic, agent <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>’s strategy <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> at current time <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> aims to maximize the sum of expected rewards from the current time step to the end, formulated as:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{s_{n}}\sum^{T}_{\tau=t}{E}[r_{s_{n}(\tau)}|R_{n}(\tau),\mathcal{M}]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></munder><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{s_{n}}\sum^{T}_{\tau=t}{E}[r_{s_{n}(\tau)}|R_{n}(\tau),\mathcal{M}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which implies that non-myopic agents may strategically sacrifice the immediate benefit of the current slot for their own long-term gain. This requires jointly considering her possible exploration outcomes and the future option information shared by others under the communication mechanism. We naturally model this as a dynamic Bayesian game with hidden information evolving over time, where the strategies of agents affect each other and should be interactively determined for the stable outcome of Bayesian equilibrium.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In our HILL problem, the platform or social planner cannot force each agent to adopt a cooperative strategy, but can apply a decentralized communication mechanism <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> in the platform. The social planner’s goal is to maximize the total time reward among all <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> agents as the social welfare by properly designing <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and predicting agents’ best response strategies to each other under <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> over time <span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            One may further apply a discount factor to future rewards for non-myopic agents in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.2. Problem formulation as MA-MDP ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) or the social welfare in (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.2. Problem formulation as MA-MDP ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), and our following analysis still works. Intuitively, a shorter time horizon <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of our current model implies a larger time discount.</span></span></span>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><mi>max</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\;{V_{\mathcal{M}}}=\max_{\mathcal{M}}\sum^{T}_{\tau=0}\sum^{N}_{n=1}{E}[r_{s_{n}(\tau)}|R_{n}(\tau),\mathcal{M}]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></munder><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\;{V_{\mathcal{M}}}=\max_{\mathcal{M}}\sum^{T}_{\tau=0}\sum^{N}_{n=1}{E}[r_{s_{n}(\tau)}|R_{n}(\tau),\mathcal{M}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s.t." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s.t.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\;s^{*}_{n}=\begin{cases}\arg\max_{s_{n}}(\ref{Myoobj}),\text{if }n\in\mathcal{N}\text{ myopic},\\
\arg\max_{s_{n}}(\ref{nonmyoobj}),\text{if }n\in\mathcal{N}\text{ non-myopic}.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.2. Problem formulation as MA-MDP ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> myopic</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.2. Problem formulation as MA-MDP ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> non-myopic</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\;s^{*}_{n}=\begin{cases}\arg\max_{s_{n}}(\ref{Myoobj}),\text{if }n\in\mathcal{N}\text{ myopic},\\
\arg\max_{s_{n}}(\ref{nonmyoobj}),\text{if }n\in\mathcal{N}\text{ non-myopic}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We next formulate our HILL problem for non-myopic agents as a MA-MDP to solve the agents’ optimal strategy under any mechanism <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the problem for myopic agents can be viewed as a special case of such formulation. First, we observe that because the reward is deterministic, agent <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> will always choose the best-known option when she decides to exploit. Therefore, in her information set <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{n}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{n}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, only the maximum reward and the corresponding option, denoted as <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bar{a}_{n}(t),m_{n}(t))=\bigg(\underset{k\in I_{n}(t)}{\arg\max}\{r_{k}\},\max_{k\in I_{n}(t)}\{r_{k}\}\bigg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">I</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bar{a}_{n}(t),m_{n}(t))=\bigg(\underset{k\in I_{n}(t)}{\arg\max}\{r_{k}\},\max_{k\in I_{n}(t)}\{r_{k}\}\bigg)</annotation></semantics></math> will influence her actions. We next introduce our formulation of MA-MDP as follows:</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">States and environment</span>: Agent <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>’s state is the maximum explored reward <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{n}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{n}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{n}(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{n}(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math> initially. The communication state acts as the environment in this MA-MDP, where <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\notin\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\notin\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates active communication and <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates blocked communication.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Action and reward</span>: Regarding option choice strategy, given <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bar{a}_{n}(t),m_{n}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bar{a}_{n}(t),m_{n}(t))</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math>, agent <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> will either exploit the currently best option <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{a}_{n}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{a}_{n}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> to receive reward <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{n}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{n}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> or randomly explore a new option outside <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{n}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{n}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> to receive expected reward <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, if <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\notin\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\notin\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, agent <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> needs to decide an information sharing policy to share what option-reward information with all other agents.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">State transition probability</span>: For agent <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> with state <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{n}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{n}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, the space of the next state <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{n}(t+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{n}(t+1)</annotation></semantics></math> is any continuous value in <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[m_{n}(t),1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[m_{n}(t),1]</annotation></semantics></math>. If she exploits <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{a}_{n}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{a}_{n}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{n}(t+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{n}(t+1)</annotation></semantics></math> remains unchanged. Conversely, if she explores a new option, her state of maximum reward transitions to <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{n}(t+1)=\max(r,m_{n}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{n}(t+1)=\max(r,m_{n}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\sim F(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\sim F(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the random variable of the option reward. If <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\notin\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\notin\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, her state transition further becomes <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{j\in\mathcal{N}_{t}\cup\{n\}}(m_{j}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{j\in\mathcal{N}_{t}\cup\{n\}}(m_{j}(t))</annotation></semantics></math> under exploitation, and <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max(r,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max(r,x)</annotation></semantics></math> whe-re <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=\max_{j\in\mathcal{N}_{t}\cup\{n\}}{(m_{j}(t))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=\max_{j\in\mathcal{N}_{t}\cup\{n\}}{(m_{j}(t))}</annotation></semantics></math> under exploration. Based on this process, we summarize the state transition probability as follows. When <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}(t)\in I_{n}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}(t)\in I_{n}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{n}(t+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{n}(t+1)</annotation></semantics></math> equals <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{n}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{n}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> with probability 1. Denote <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{t}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> as the CDF of any other agent <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>’s shared state <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{j}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{j}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> to agent <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> at the end of <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\notin\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\notin\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. The PDF of the rest state transitions is:</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p(m_{n}(t+1)=m|a_{n}(t),t,m_{n}(t))=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(m_{n}(t+1)=m|a_{n}(t),t,m_{n}(t))=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{cases}&amp;\!\!\!\!\frac{dF(r)G_{t}(r)^{|\mathcal{N}_{t}\cup\{n\}|}}{dr}|_{r=m},\;\text{if }a_{n}(t)\notin I_{n}(t),t\notin\mathcal{M},\\
&amp;\!\!\!\!\frac{dF(r)}{dr}|_{r=m},\qquad\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\ \text{ if }a_{n}(t)\notin I_{n}(t),t\in\mathcal{M},\\
&amp;\!\!\!\!\frac{dG_{t}(r)^{|\mathcal{N}_{t}\cup\{n\}|}}{dr}|_{r=m},\ \;\;\;\;\text{ if }a_{n}(t)\in I_{n}(t),t\notin\mathcal{M}.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="5.187em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="1.787em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{cases}&amp;\!\!\!\!\frac{dF(r)G_{t}(r)^{|\mathcal{N}_{t}\cup\{n\}|}}{dr}|_{r=m},\;\text{if }a_{n}(t)\notin I_{n}(t),t\notin\mathcal{M},\\
&amp;\!\!\!\!\frac{dF(r)}{dr}|_{r=m},\qquad\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\ \text{ if }a_{n}(t)\notin I_{n}(t),t\in\mathcal{M},\\
&amp;\!\!\!\!\frac{dG_{t}(r)^{|\mathcal{N}_{t}\cup\{n\}|}}{dr}|_{r=m},\ \;\;\;\;\text{ if }a_{n}(t)\in I_{n}(t),t\notin\mathcal{M}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">For the MA-MDP described above, the Bellman equation for agent <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>’s optimal long-term reward <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{n}(t\mid m_{n}(t),\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{n}(t\mid m_{n}(t),\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, under the state <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{n}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{n}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and the communication environment <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, is expressed as:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle U_{n}(t|m_{n}(t),\mathcal{M})=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle U_{n}(t|m_{n}(t),\mathcal{M})=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!\!\!\!\begin{cases}&amp;\!\!\!\!\max(\mu+E[U_{n}(t+1|\max(r,\max_{j\in\mathcal{N}_{t}\cup\{n\}}m_{j}(t)),\mathcal{M})],\\
&amp;\ m_{n}(t)+E[U_{n}(t+1|\max(\max_{j\in\mathcal{N}_{t}\cup\{n\}}m_{j}(t)),\mathcal{M})]),\\
&amp;\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\quad\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\text{ if }t\notin\mathcal{M},\\
&amp;\!\!\!\!\max(\mu+E[U_{n}(t+1|\max(r,m_{n}(t)),\mathcal{M})],\\
&amp;\ m_{n}(t)+E[U_{n}(t+1|m_{n}(t),\mathcal{M})]),\ \text{ if }t\in\mathcal{M},\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>U</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>max</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>max</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>m</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0.500em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>U</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>max</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0.500em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!\!\!\!\begin{cases}&amp;\!\!\!\!\max(\mu+E[U_{n}(t+1|\max(r,\max_{j\in\mathcal{N}_{t}\cup\{n\}}m_{j}(t)),\mathcal{M})],\\
&amp;\ m_{n}(t)+E[U_{n}(t+1|\max(\max_{j\in\mathcal{N}_{t}\cup\{n\}}m_{j}(t)),\mathcal{M})]),\\
&amp;\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\quad\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\text{ if }t\notin\mathcal{M},\\
&amp;\!\!\!\!\max(\mu+E[U_{n}(t+1|\max(r,m_{n}(t)),\mathcal{M})],\\
&amp;\ m_{n}(t)+E[U_{n}(t+1|m_{n}(t),\mathcal{M})]),\ \text{ if }t\in\mathcal{M},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">which also depends on other agents’ states and strategies, influenced by her information-sharing strategy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">By backward induction in this multi-agent MDP, we can identify that the optimal option choice strategy <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{n}(t|R_{n}(t),\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{n}(t|R_{n}(t),\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> must be a threshold-based strategy to decide to explore or exploit, as demonstrated in the following lemma:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Lemma 2.1.</h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under any communication mechanism <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, each agent <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>’s optimal option choice strategy <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{n}(t|m_{n}(t),\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{n}(t|m_{n}(t),\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\!\in\![T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\!\in\![T]</annotation></semantics></math> follows an exploration threshold sequence <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> over time. That is, she will explore a new option if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{n}(t)&lt;u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{n}(t)&lt;u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and otherwise stick with the currently best option <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{a}_{n}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{a}_{n}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In this MA-MDP, each agent has two actions: exploration/exploi-tation and information sharing to decide. Solving <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> for non-myopic agents is challenging because their exploration thresholds are influenced by the potential information exchanged from others in any future <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\notin\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\notin\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, they tend to reduce exploration efforts when anticipating useful reward messages from others in the near future. This requires consideration of these two-dimensional actions to jointly determine all agents’ exploration thresholds <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and information sharing strategy in the equilibrium of the dynamic Bayesian game under the communication mechanism <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to non-myopic agents, myopic agents have a straightforward strategy, with a constant threshold <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}=\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}=\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, as they only care about the current time slot’s reward. However, the dynamic exploration process of myopic agents under various possible communication mechanisms remains complicated to analyze. We will show later that designing the optimal decentralized communication mechanism for myopic agents is already an NP-hard problem to solve.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, based on the MA-MDP framework, we will address the questions of whether and when decentralized communication can be more advantageous than the current practice of centralized communications for myopic agents in the platform. Then we will design a decentralized communication mechanism to maximize social welfare (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.2. Problem formulation as MA-MDP ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) by solving an NP-hard problem.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will first examine the centralized communications policy to give all agents open access to their shared information, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. We begin by presenting the optimal learning strategy of each myopic agent <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> for maximizing her immediate reward in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.2. Problem formulation as MA-MDP ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) in the dynamic Bayesian game. We solve the exploration threshold <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2.2. Problem formulation as MA-MDP ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, the weakly dominant information sharing strategy, and social welfare below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Lemma 3.1.</h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">At each time <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math>, each myopic agent <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>’s option exploration threshold sequence is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1}=\ldots=u_{T}=\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}=\ldots=u_{T}=\mu</annotation></semantics></math>. Once permitted to communicate with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\notin\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\notin\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, myopic agents will truthfully share their latest information. Then the long-term social welfare in (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.2. Problem formulation as MA-MDP ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) is:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{\emptyset}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{\emptyset}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N(T+1)\int^{1}_{\mu}rd\frac{F(r)^{N}-F(\mu)^{N}}{1-F(\mu)^{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N(T+1)\int^{1}_{\mu}rd\frac{F(r)^{N}-F(\mu)^{N}}{1-F(\mu)^{N}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle-N\frac{1-F(\mu)^{N(T+1)}}{1-F(\mu)^{N}}\int^{1}_{\mu}\frac{1-F(r)^{N}}{1-F(\mu)^{N}}dr," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∫</mo></mstyle><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-N\frac{1-F(\mu)^{N(T+1)}}{1-F(\mu)^{N}}\int^{1}_{\mu}\frac{1-F(r)^{N}}{1-F(\mu)^{N}}dr,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where agents quickly stop exploring any new option with expected exploration time:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(1-F(\mu)^{N(T+1)})/(1-F(\mu)^{N})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(1-F(\mu)^{N(T+1)})/(1-F(\mu)^{N}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> is given in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Proof of Lemma ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. Myopic agents’ centralized communications enable the community to converge quickly once some agents find an option with reward larger than threshold <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>. This fast convergence in (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) implies a lack of essential exploration with poor performance in (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), particularly for a large number of options available. Additionally, given that agents’ communication takes place after their options are chosen, agent <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>’s misreporting of her latest information <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{n}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{n}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> does not alter her immediate reward in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.2. Problem formulation as MA-MDP ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Thus, she is willing to truthfully share the information. Later in Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Dcentralized Communication Mechanism for Non-myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we will show that this is not the case for non-myopic users who may withhold information to gain in the long run.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To identify the sufficient and necessary conditions under which centralized communications should be replaced by decentralized communication, we first meticulously choose a special decentralized communication pattern with a single no-communication time window <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{M}}=\{T_{1},\ldots,T_{1}+\Delta_{1}-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{M}}=\{T_{1},\ldots,T_{1}+\Delta_{1}-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> to compare with centralized communications. We derive the long-term social welfare objective under <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{\bar{\mathcal{M}}}=NF(\mu)^{NT_{1}}\bigg((T-T_{1}-\Delta_{1})y_{\Delta_{1}+1}-\sum_{i=1}^{\Delta_{1}}x_{i}\bigg)+V_{\emptyset}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{\bar{\mathcal{M}}}=NF(\mu)^{NT_{1}}\bigg((T-T_{1}-\Delta_{1})y_{\Delta_{1}+1}-\sum_{i=1}^{\Delta_{1}}x_{i}\bigg)+V_{\emptyset},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{i}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{i}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int^{1}_{\mu}\bigg(\frac{F(r)-F(\mu)}{1-F(\mu)}+F(\mu)^{i}\frac{1-F(r)}{1-F(\mu)}\bigg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int^{1}_{\mu}\bigg(\frac{F(r)-F(\mu)}{1-F(\mu)}+F(\mu)^{i}\frac{1-F(r)}{1-F(\mu)}\bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\bigg(\frac{F(r)^{N}-F(\mu)^{N}}{1-F(\mu)^{N}}+F(\mu)^{iN}\frac{1-F(r)^{N}}{1-F(\mu)^{N}}\bigg)dr," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\bigg(\frac{F(r)^{N}-F(\mu)^{N}}{1-F(\mu)^{N}}+F(\mu)^{iN}\frac{1-F(r)^{N}}{1-F(\mu)^{N}}\bigg)dr,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y_{i}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y_{i}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int^{1}_{\mu}\bigg(\frac{F(r)^{N}-F(\mu)^{N}}{1-F(\mu)^{N}}+F(\mu)^{iN}\frac{1-F(r)^{N}}{1-F(\mu)^{N}}\bigg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int^{1}_{\mu}\bigg(\frac{F(r)^{N}-F(\mu)^{N}}{1-F(\mu)^{N}}+F(\mu)^{iN}\frac{1-F(r)^{N}}{1-F(\mu)^{N}}\bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\bigg(\frac{F(r)-F(\mu)}{1-F(\mu)}+F(\mu)^{i}\frac{1-F(r)}{1-F(\mu)}\bigg)^{N}dr," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\bigg(\frac{F(r)-F(\mu)}{1-F(\mu)}+F(\mu)^{i}\frac{1-F(r)}{1-F(\mu)}\bigg)^{N}dr,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,\ldots,\Delta_{1}+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,\ldots,\Delta_{1}+1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\emptyset}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\emptyset}</annotation></semantics></math> is given in (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). Here <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> can be interpreted as the reward loss due to no-communication among agents to share and exploit the currently best options, while <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> tells the benefit of boosting self-exploration using no-communication for the future <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-T_{1}-\Delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-T_{1}-\Delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> time slots.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Equation (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) suggests that if its bracket <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T-T_{1}-\Delta_{1})y_{\Delta_{1}+1}-\sum_{i=1}^{\Delta_{1}}x_{i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T-T_{1}-\Delta_{1})y_{\Delta_{1}+1}-\sum_{i=1}^{\Delta_{1}}x_{i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, decentralized communication <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> outperforms centralized communications, which provide us a sufficient condition for decentralized communication mechanism <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> to outperform. Intriguingly, we find that it is also necessary for the existence of decentralized communication mechanisms to outperform centralized communication. This can be done by showing that our later derivation of social welfare in (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) under general decentralized communication pattern <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\cup_{m=1}^{M}\{T_{m},\ldots,T_{m}+\Delta_{m}-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∪</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\cup_{m=1}^{M}\{T_{m},\ldots,T_{m}+\Delta_{m}-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> degenerates to the particular <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> in the comparison with the centralized communications’ <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\emptyset}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\emptyset}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Proposition 3.2.</h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Both the sufficient and necessary condition for our decentralized communication mechanism to outperform the centralized communications policy is given below in closed-form:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T&gt;\min_{\Delta\in\{1,\ldots,T-1\}}\left\{\frac{\sum^{\Delta}_{i=1}x_{i}}{y_{\Delta+1}}+\Delta\right\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T&gt;\min_{\Delta\in\{1,\ldots,T-1\}}\left\{\frac{\sum^{\Delta}_{i=1}x_{i}}{y_{\Delta+1}}+\Delta\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 3.2. ‣ 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> is given in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Proof of Proposition ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In Proposition 3.2. ‣ 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) indicates that as long as time horizon <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is large or the reward distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) becomes positively skewed with smaller mean <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, it is better for the platform to replace the prevailing centralized communications policy with decentralized communication to encourage each agent to self-explore and find an option with reward larger than <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>. Once communication is allowed later at <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}+\Delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}+\Delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the community picks up the best option of all agents with a reward much better than <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Decentralized Communication Mechanism</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In Proposition 3.2. ‣ 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>), we investigate how to design the best decentralized communication mechanism <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{*}_{1}=\cup_{m=1}^{M}\{T_{m}^{*},\ldots,T_{m}^{*}+\Delta_{m}^{*}-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∪</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{*}_{1}=\cup_{m=1}^{M}\{T_{m}^{*},\ldots,T_{m}^{*}+\Delta_{m}^{*}-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> for myopic agents to maximize the social welfare objective in (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.2. Problem formulation as MA-MDP ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). Yet the further derivation for equation (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.2. Problem formulation as MA-MDP ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) under any <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is complicated because agents, without frequent communication, do not converge synchronously and exhibit various case combinations by observing different option rewards in each no-communication time window. Through derivation using <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>), we have the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Lemma 3.3.</h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given any decentralized communication mechanism <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\cup_{m=1}^{M}\{T_{m},\ldots,T_{m}+\Delta_{m}-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∪</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\cup_{m=1}^{M}\{T_{m},\ldots,T_{m}+\Delta_{m}-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the expected social welfare to consider all time slots is given by:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{\mathcal{M}}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{\mathcal{M}}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N\sum^{M}_{m=1}F(\mu)^{NT_{m}}\bigg((T-T_{m}-\Delta_{m})y_{\Delta_{m}+1}-\sum_{i=1}^{\Delta_{m}}x_{i}\bigg)+V_{\emptyset}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N\sum^{M}_{m=1}F(\mu)^{NT_{m}}\bigg((T-T_{m}-\Delta_{m})y_{\Delta_{m}+1}-\sum_{i=1}^{\Delta_{m}}x_{i}\bigg)+V_{\emptyset},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\emptyset}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\emptyset}</annotation></semantics></math> is given in (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). The expected number of exploration is increased from (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) under centralized communications to</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum^{T_{1}}_{t=0}F(\mu)^{Nt}+\sum^{M}_{m=1}\bigg(\sum^{T_{m+1}}_{t=T_{m}+\Delta_{m}+1}\!\!\!\!\!\!F(\mu)^{Nt}+\sum^{\Delta_{m}}_{t=1}F(\mu)^{NT_{m}+t}\bigg)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum^{T_{1}}_{t=0}F(\mu)^{Nt}+\sum^{M}_{m=1}\bigg(\sum^{T_{m+1}}_{t=T_{m}+\Delta_{m}+1}\!\!\!\!\!\!F(\mu)^{Nt}+\sum^{\Delta_{m}}_{t=1}F(\mu)^{NT_{m}+t}\bigg).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> is given in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Proof for Lemma ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>. In (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), when we extend the no-communication duration from <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{m}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{m}+1</annotation></semantics></math> in mechanism <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, we incur immediate exploitation loss of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\Delta_{m}+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\Delta_{m}+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, yet the future exploration benefit increases from <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\Delta_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\Delta_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\Delta_{m}+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\Delta_{m}+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We need to balance them well to decide the optimal timing of no-communication.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), we can formulate the optimization problem to determine the optimal decentralized communication mechanism <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{*}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{*}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. This problem is NP-hard due to the inclusion of integer timing decisions for disabling communication among agents and a non-convex objective. However, equation (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) decreases with <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and this feature helps simplify the optimization problem, as detailed in the subsequent lemma:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Lemma 3.4.</h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The optimal decentralized communication mechanism <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{*}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{*}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>=<math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\cup_{m=1}^{M}\{T_{m}^{*},\ldots," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cup_{m=1}^{M}\{T_{m}^{*},\ldots,</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T_{m}^{*}+\Delta_{m}^{*}-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{m}^{*}+\Delta_{m}^{*}-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> must satisfy <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{*}_{1}=0,T^{*}_{m+1}=T^{*}_{m}+\Delta^{*}_{m}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{*}_{1}=0,T^{*}_{m+1}=T^{*}_{m}+\Delta^{*}_{m}+1</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\{1,\ldots,M-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\{1,\ldots,M-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the optimal mechanism, we need to disable communication initially with <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}^{*}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}^{*}=0</annotation></semantics></math> to encourage agents’ own exploration. Additionally, we only include a single communication round between two neighboring no-communication windows, as it suffices for agents to share their option information. Then the problem of jointly deciding <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{m}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{m}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{m}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{m}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is simplified to deciding <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{m}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{m}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> only, and the optimization problem can be simplified as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{M,\{\Delta_{m}\}_{m=1}^{M}}N\bigg((T-\Delta_{1})y_{\Delta_{1}+1}-\sum_{i=1}^{\Delta_{1}}x_{i}\bigg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{M,\{\Delta_{m}\}_{m=1}^{M}}N\bigg((T-\Delta_{1})y_{\Delta_{1}+1}-\sum_{i=1}^{\Delta_{1}}x_{i}\bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle+\sum^{M}_{m=2}NF(\mu)^{N\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}(\Delta_{i}+1)}\bigg((T-\sum_{i=1}^{m}\Delta_{i}-m+1)y_{\Delta_{m}+1}-\sum_{i=1}^{\Delta_{m}}x_{i}\bigg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>N</mi><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\sum^{M}_{m=2}NF(\mu)^{N\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}(\Delta_{i}+1)}\bigg((T-\sum_{i=1}^{m}\Delta_{i}-m+1)y_{\Delta_{m}+1}-\sum_{i=1}^{\Delta_{m}}x_{i}\bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\quad\quad\text{s.t. }\;\Delta_{i}\in{Z}^{+}\;,\forall i\in\{1,\ldots,M\},\;\sum_{i=1}^{M}\Delta_{i}\leq T-M," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s.t. </mtext><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\quad\quad\text{s.t. }\;\Delta_{i}\in{Z}^{+}\;,\forall i\in\{1,\ldots,M\},\;\sum_{i=1}^{M}\Delta_{i}\leq T-M,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(14)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\;\;\quad\quad\quad\quad\;M\in\{1,\ldots,\lfloor T/2\rfloor\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\;\;\quad\quad\quad\quad\;M\in\{1,\ldots,\lfloor T/2\rfloor\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">However, this problem is still a difficult non-convex problem to solve with combinatorial decisions in <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\Delta_{1},\ldots,\Delta_{M}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\Delta_{1},\ldots,\Delta_{M}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. While a recursive algorithm can be developed for it, allowing for some pruning of the search space based on condition (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In Proposition 3.2. ‣ 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>), this algorithm still runs in exponential time and it is hard to optimally solve for non-small horizon <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To tell what the optimal mechanism looks like, we use an illustrative example with <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=30</annotation></semantics></math> for affordable computation. We opt for the Beta distribution as the prior <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and run two experiments with mean option rewards of <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.024</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.024</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.33" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.33</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.33</annotation></semantics></math>. Their corresponding optimal mechanisms are depicted in Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The no-communication window duration decreases over time for <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=0.024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=0.024</annotation></semantics></math>, as less time remains, reducing the need for long communication windows to encourage exploration. Additionally, as <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> increases from 0.024 to 0.33, both the number <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> of no-communication windows and their durations <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> decrease. As the mean reward <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> increases, myopic agents have a higher exploration threshold, and the system with centralized communications incurs less under-exploration. Thus, the optimal mechanism requires fewer no-communication windows.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09574v1/figures/RCMOPT.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/46;" width="238" height="46" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>Optimal decentralized communication mechanism for 2 different Beta distributions with means 0.024 and 0.33, respectively.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For the tractability of solving problem (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>), we reduce it to an approximate version below that concentrates on a single no-communication window <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{M}}_{1}=\{0,\ldots,\Delta_{1}-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{M}}_{1}=\{0,\ldots,\Delta_{1}-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, inspired by observing (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) that the benefit from the <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-th no-communication window is discounted by <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\mu)^{N\sum^{m-1}_{i=1}(\Delta_{i}+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\mu)^{N\sum^{m-1}_{i=1}(\Delta_{i}+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, exponentially diminishing over <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. By setting <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}^{*}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}^{*}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), the approximate version of problem (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) is:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(15)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{\Delta_{1}\in\{1,\ldots,T-1\}}N\bigg((T-\Delta_{1})y_{\Delta_{1}+1}-\sum_{i=1}^{\Delta_{1}}x_{i}\bigg)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{\Delta_{1}\in\{1,\ldots,T-1\}}N\bigg((T-\Delta_{1})y_{\Delta_{1}+1}-\sum_{i=1}^{\Delta_{1}}x_{i}\bigg).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">Consequently, we propose Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> to solve this approximate problem efficiently. At the beginning of the search, the algorithm first checks the assumption (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In Proposition 3.2. ‣ 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) on the total time horizon <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to disable communication. If (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In Proposition 3.2. ‣ 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) is satisfied, it iterates over all <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{1}\in\{1,\ldots,T-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{1}\in\{1,\ldots,T-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and returns the one <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{1}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{1}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> for which mechanism <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{M}}_{1}^{*}=\{0,\ldots,\Delta_{1}^{*}-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{M}}_{1}^{*}=\{0,\ldots,\Delta_{1}^{*}-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> yields the maximum social welfare <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\tilde{\mathcal{M}}_{1}^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\tilde{\mathcal{M}}_{1}^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Asymptotically Optimal Decentralized Communication Mechanism <math id="alg1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{M}}_{1}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{M}}_{1}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l0.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
              
             <math id="alg1.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\leq\min_{\Delta_{1}\in\{1,\ldots,T-1\}}\left\{\frac{\sum^{\Delta_{1}}_{i=1}x_{i}}{y_{\Delta_{1}+1}}+\Delta_{1}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup></mstyle><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\leq\min_{\Delta_{1}\in\{1,\ldots,T-1\}}\left\{\frac{\sum^{\Delta_{1}}_{i=1}x_{i}}{y_{\Delta_{1}+1}}+\Delta_{1}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In Proposition 3.2. ‣ 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) <span id="alg1.l1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span>  <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{1}^{*}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{1}^{*}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for centralized communications

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{1}=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\tilde{\mathcal{M}}_{1}^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\tilde{\mathcal{M}}_{1}^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) is greater than <math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{V}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
               <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{1}^{*}=\Delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{1}^{*}=\Delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{M}}_{1}^{*}=\{0,\ldots,\Delta_{1}^{*}-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{M}}_{1}^{*}=\{0,\ldots,\Delta_{1}^{*}-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{V}=V_{\tilde{\mathcal{M}}_{1}^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{V}=V_{\tilde{\mathcal{M}}_{1}^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span>  <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{M}}^{*}_{1}=\{0,\ldots,\Delta_{1}^{*}-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{M}}^{*}_{1}=\{0,\ldots,\Delta_{1}^{*}-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Theorem 3.5.</h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> operates with a linear complexity of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T)</annotation></semantics></math> and guarantees that the social welfare, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\tilde{\mathcal{M}}_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\tilde{\mathcal{M}}_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, achieved by the approximate mechanism, is a <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\frac{F(\mu)^{2N}-F(\mu)^{TN}}{1-F(\mu)^{TN}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\frac{F(\mu)^{2N}-F(\mu)^{TN}}{1-F(\mu)^{TN}})</annotation></semantics></math>-approximation of the social optimum <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathcal{M}^{*}_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathcal{M}^{*}_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> for problem (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>). Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is asymptotically optimal as <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, and leads to a linear-order social welfare gain from (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) in centralized communications over time.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5. ‣ 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> is given in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Proof of Theorem ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>. Note that even as <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently large, the centralized communications policy remains non-optimal by (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In Proposition 3.2. ‣ 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We now run simulations to examine Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> besides the theoretical results in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5. ‣ 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>. We run Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> across various Beta distributions for option distribution with different parameter settings of mean reward value <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.6</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We examine the performance gain <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\tilde{\mathcal{M}}_{1}^{*}}-V_{\emptyset}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\tilde{\mathcal{M}}_{1}^{*}}-V_{\emptyset}</annotation></semantics></math> of Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> from centralized communications versus <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> in Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, and it also shows the upper bound of optimal performance gain <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{{\mathcal{M}}_{1}^{*}}-V_{\emptyset}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{{\mathcal{M}}_{1}^{*}}-V_{\emptyset}</annotation></semantics></math> since the optimum is difficult to solve for large <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> in hundreds. Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows that our performance gain <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\tilde{\mathcal{M}}_{1}^{*}}-V_{\emptyset}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\tilde{\mathcal{M}}_{1}^{*}}-V_{\emptyset}</annotation></semantics></math> scales up linearly with <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, in accordance with Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5. ‣ 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>. Additionally, even for finite <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=100</annotation></semantics></math> as used by Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the social welfare achieved by our asymptotically optimal mechanism is almost the same as the upper bound of the optimum. Notably, our mechanism’s performance gain increases as <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> decreases, since the centralized communications benchmark encourages agents to converge more quickly to lower exploration threshold <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.09574v1/Sim_my_F_M-eps-converted-to.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/104;" width="143" height="104"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>Performance gains of Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2. Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and upper bound of optimum from centralized communications, versus <math id="S3.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> of 0.3, 0.5 and 0.6.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Dcentralized Communication Mechanism for Non-myopic Agents</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we extend to consider non-myopic human agents who may strategically sacrifice the immediate benefit of the current time slot for her own long-term reward in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.2. Problem formulation as MA-MDP ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). After analyzing non-myopic agents’ strategy under MA-MDP game, we develop a new decentralized communication mechanism strategically tailored for non-myopic agents.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Analysis of Existing Centralized Communications Policy</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first examine non-myopic agents’ information communication strategy under centralized communications of the dynamic Bayesian game, which is different from that of myopic agents in Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. Non-myopic agents, to maximize long-term rewards in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.2. Problem formulation as MA-MDP ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), consider all potential outcomes in their strategic choices in the MA-MDP game of Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.1. System Model on HILL Game ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and may not truthfully share information with others. The imperfect and incomplete information in this game arises from agents not knowing how many will share at each time <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and being unaware of other agents’ <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{n}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{n}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. However, based on the following weakly dominant strategy for information sharing, the Bayesian perfect equilibrium in the dynamic Bayesian game can be identified.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Lemma 4.1.</h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In each time slot <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math> with centralized communications, it is a perfect Bayesian equilibrium for each agent <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> to withhold any useful information until time slot <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and to share truthful information in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-1</annotation></semantics></math> with any probability.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If agent <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> believes that another agent <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is willing to share their maximum reward information at time <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> with a positive probability <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, the best response for agent <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is to withhold sharing before <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> with agent <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> to maximize the expected reward information received from <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, as determined by problem (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.2. Problem formulation as MA-MDP ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). If this belief is 0 for all time slots, withholding information remains a weakly dominant strategy for agent <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, as sharing would not impact the expected information from <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, given <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> would not share in return. Consequently, withholding information until the final communication round forms an equilibrium in the information-sharing strategy, as agents no longer have the opportunity to benefit from others’ exploration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">At time <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-1</annotation></semantics></math>, since sharing probabilities in <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> for all agents are weakly dominant strategies, infinitely many perfect Bayesian equilibria exist, corresponding to various combinations of sharing probability for each agent. However, the equilibrium where every agent shares with a probability of 1 at <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-1</annotation></semantics></math> is Pareto optimal. Thus, we focus on this specific perfect Bayesian equilibrium for further analysis, where <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{T-1}=\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{T-1}=\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{t}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{t}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&lt;T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&lt;T-1</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.2. Problem formulation as MA-MDP ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.2. Problem formulation as MA-MDP ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">At the Pareto optimum of the equilibrium, agents will expect to use other’s information at time <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-1</annotation></semantics></math> to improve their final reward at the last time slot <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> based on the reward function (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.2. Problem formulation as MA-MDP ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). Before time slot <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-1</annotation></semantics></math>, agent
<math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> forms a belief <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{T-1}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{T-1}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> regarding the maximum reward <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}(T-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}(T-1)</annotation></semantics></math> shared by any other agent <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq n</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-1</annotation></semantics></math>, which should match with her own distribution of <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{n}(T-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{n}(T-1)</annotation></semantics></math> in the Bayesian equilibrium. Besides the information communication strategy, they will further decide their option choice strategy based on this belief before time <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-1</annotation></semantics></math> and (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.2. Problem formulation as MA-MDP ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), following the exploration threshold sequence <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> in Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2.2. Problem formulation as MA-MDP ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Lemma 4.2.</h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under centralized communications, non-myopic agents’ exploration threshold sequence <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly decreasing with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1}&gt;\ldots&gt;u_{T}=\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}&gt;\ldots&gt;u_{T}=\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, which are unique solutions to:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(16)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u_{t}-\mu=\begin{cases}&amp;\!\!\!\!\!(T-t-1)(\int^{1}_{u_{t}}1-F(r)dr)+\\
&amp;\!\!\!\!\!\int^{1}_{u_{t}}G_{T-1}(r)^{N-1}(1-F(r))dr,\;\text{if }t=1,\ldots,T-1,\\
&amp;\!\!\!\!\!0,\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\;\;\;\;\;\;\text{if }t=T,\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">d</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">d</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="11.847em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{t}-\mu=\begin{cases}&amp;\!\!\!\!\!(T-t-1)(\int^{1}_{u_{t}}1-F(r)dr)+\\
&amp;\!\!\!\!\!\int^{1}_{u_{t}}G_{T-1}(r)^{N-1}(1-F(r))dr,\;\text{if }t=1,\ldots,T-1,\\
&amp;\!\!\!\!\!0,\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\;\;\;\;\;\;\text{if }t=T,\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{T-1}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{T-1}(r)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the distribution of each agent <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>’s maximum reward <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{n}(T-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{n}(T-1)</annotation></semantics></math> by time <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-1</annotation></semantics></math> determined by <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T-1}</annotation></semantics></math> in the following:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.EGx16.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.EGx16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{T-1}(r)=\left\{\begin{array}[]{l@{\;}l}F(r)^{t}-(1-F(r))\sum^{T-1}_{i=t}F(u_{i})^{i},&amp;\text{if }u_{t}&lt;r\leq u_{t-1}\\
&amp;\;\;\text{ with }u_{0}=1,\\
&amp;\;\;\;\text{and }1\leq t\leq T-1,\\
F(r)^{T},&amp;\text{if }r\leq u_{T-1}.\end{array}\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> with </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{T-1}(r)=\left\{\begin{array}[]{l@{\;}l}F(r)^{t}-(1-F(r))\sum^{T-1}_{i=t}F(u_{i})^{i},&amp;\text{if }u_{t}&lt;r\leq u_{t-1}\\
&amp;\;\;\text{ with }u_{0}=1,\\
&amp;\;\;\;\text{and }1\leq t\leq T-1,\\
F(r)^{T},&amp;\text{if }r\leq u_{T-1}.\end{array}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Different from myopic agents’ insufficient exploration time in (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), non-myopic agents will over-explore with a longer expected number of explorations:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(21)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1+F(u_{T})^{TN}+\sum_{t=1}^{T-1}F(u_{t})^{t}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1+F(u_{T})^{TN}+\sum_{t=1}^{T-1}F(u_{t})^{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 4.2. ‣ 4.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communications Policy ‣ 4. Dcentralized Communication Mechanism for Non-myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> is given in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Proof for Lemma ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>. The threshold equation (<a href="#S4.E16" title="In Lemma 4.2. ‣ 4.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communications Policy ‣ 4. Dcentralized Communication Mechanism for Non-myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) is derived from backward induction in MA-MDP described in Section <a href="#S2" title="2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> using equations (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.2. Problem formulation as MA-MDP ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). Each <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the value that agent <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is indifferent between long-term reward under actions of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}(t)\notin I_{n}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}(t)\notin I_{n}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{a}_{n}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{a}_{n}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Compared to the myopic agents’ constant exploration threshold <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, non-myopic agents increase their threshold to <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&lt;T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&lt;T</annotation></semantics></math>, targeting better options for future exploitation and larger long-term reward in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.2. Problem formulation as MA-MDP ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The sequence <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is decreasing in <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, as agents should explore less for shorter futures. Moreover, non-myopic agents allocate more exploration time slots in (<a href="#S4.E21" title="In Lemma 4.2. ‣ 4.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communications Policy ‣ 4. Dcentralized Communication Mechanism for Non-myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>) than myopic agents’ (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communication Policy ‣ 3. Decentralized Communication Mechanism for Myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). In fact, while myopic agents with effective communications tend to under-explore under the centralized communications policy, non-myopic agents selfishly choosing not to share can only rely on their own and thus over-explore at the equilibrium. Although non-myopic agents prioritize long-term rewards and intensify their exploration efforts, the overall performance of social welfare remains poor due to their practice of withholding information. Later in Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Simulation Experiments with Real Dataset for Stochastic Rewards and Heterogeneous Preference ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we will show that our decentralized communication mechanism obviously outperforms the centralized policy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Optimal Decentralized Communication Mechanism</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 4.1. ‣ 4.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communications Policy ‣ 4. Dcentralized Communication Mechanism for Non-myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> still holds similarly for any decentralized communication, where agents will only truthfully share information in the last communication time slot under any <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then in the Pareto optimum, any <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> reduces to include only a single communication time slot <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> due to non-myopic agents’ information withholding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We denote the exploration threshold sequence to <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{t}(T_{1})\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{t}(T_{1})\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> since it depends on <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in the reduced one-time communication mechanism <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}=[T]\setminus\{T_{1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}=[T]\setminus\{T_{1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The centralized communications policy is a special case of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}=T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}=T-1</annotation></semantics></math> for outputting the monotonic threshold sequence in (<a href="#S4.E16" title="In Lemma 4.2. ‣ 4.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communications Policy ‣ 4. Dcentralized Communication Mechanism for Non-myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>). The threshold sequence <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{t}(T_{1})\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{t}(T_{1})\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> can be solved similarly using backward induction under equation (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.2. Problem formulation as MA-MDP ‣ 2. System Model and Problem Formulation ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) in the MA-MDP. The key difference between <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{t}(T-1)\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{t}(T-1)\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{t}(T_{1})\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{t}(T_{1})\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is that <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{t}(T_{1})\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{t}(T_{1})\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> may not monotonically decrease. Intuitively, agents might halt exploration before <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to take advantage of others’ option sharing at <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and resume exploration after <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> if the shared information does not meet their expectations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Proposition 4.3.</h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any one-time communication mechanism <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}=[T]\setminus\{T_{1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}=[T]\setminus\{T_{1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the non-myopic agents’ exploration threshold sequence <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{t}(T_{1})\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{t}(T_{1})\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> only satisfies monotonicity before and after the communication time <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1}(T_{1})&gt;u_{2}(T_{1})&gt;\ldots&gt;u_{T_{1}}(T_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}(T_{1})&gt;u_{2}(T_{1})&gt;\ldots&gt;u_{T_{1}}(T_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{T_{1}+1}(T_{1})&gt;u_{T_{1}+2}(T_{1})&gt;\ldots&gt;u_{T}(T_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{T_{1}+1}(T_{1})&gt;u_{T_{1}+2}(T_{1})&gt;\ldots&gt;u_{T}(T_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(T_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(T_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> may exceed <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(T_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(T_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\leq T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\leq T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2. Optimal Decentralized Communication Mechanism ‣ 4. Dcentralized Communication Mechanism for Non-myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> as an illustration), which are unique solutions to a system of following equations:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u_{t}(T_{1})-\mu=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{t}(T_{1})-\mu=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(22)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{cases}&amp;(T_{1}-t)\left(\int^{1}_{u_{t}(T_{1})}1-F(r)dr\right)\\
&amp;+(T-T_{1})\int^{1}_{u_{t}(T_{1})}G_{T_{1}}(r)^{N-1}(1-F(r))dr,\\
&amp;\qquad\qquad\quad\qquad\quad\quad\quad\;\text{if
}t=1,\ldots,T_{1},u_{t}(T_{1})&gt;u_{T_{1}+1}(T_{1}),\\
&amp;(T_{1}-t)\left(\int^{1}_{u_{t}(T_{1})}1-F(r)dr\right)\\
&amp;+(T-T_{1})\int^{1}_{u_{T_{1}+1}(T_{1})}G_{T_{1}}(r)^{N-1}(1-F(r))dr\\
&amp;+\sum^{T_{1}+k-1}_{j=T_{1}+1}(T-j)\int^{u_{j}(T_{1})}_{u_{j+1}(T_{1})}G_{T_{1}}(r)^{N-1}F(r)^{j-T_{1}}(1-F(r))dr\\
&amp;+(T-T_{1}-k)\int^{u_{T_{1}+k}(T_{1})}_{u_{t}(T_{1})}G_{T_{1}}(r)^{N-1}F(r)^{k}(1-F(r))dr,\\
&amp;\!\!\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\text{if
}t=1,\ldots,T_{1},u_{T_{1}+k}(T_{1})&gt;u_{t}(T_{1})&gt;u_{T_{1}+k+1}(T_{1}),\\
&amp;\!\!\;\;\;\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad k=1,\ldots,T-T_{1}-1,\\
\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">d</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∫</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">d</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∫</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∫</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∫</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{cases}&amp;(T_{1}-t)\left(\int^{1}_{u_{t}(T_{1})}1-F(r)dr\right)\\
&amp;+(T-T_{1})\int^{1}_{u_{t}(T_{1})}G_{T_{1}}(r)^{N-1}(1-F(r))dr,\\
&amp;\qquad\qquad\quad\qquad\quad\quad\quad\;\text{if
}t=1,\ldots,T_{1},u_{t}(T_{1})&gt;u_{T_{1}+1}(T_{1}),\\
&amp;(T_{1}-t)\left(\int^{1}_{u_{t}(T_{1})}1-F(r)dr\right)\\
&amp;+(T-T_{1})\int^{1}_{u_{T_{1}+1}(T_{1})}G_{T_{1}}(r)^{N-1}(1-F(r))dr\\
&amp;+\sum^{T_{1}+k-1}_{j=T_{1}+1}(T-j)\int^{u_{j}(T_{1})}_{u_{j+1}(T_{1})}G_{T_{1}}(r)^{N-1}F(r)^{j-T_{1}}(1-F(r))dr\\
&amp;+(T-T_{1}-k)\int^{u_{T_{1}+k}(T_{1})}_{u_{t}(T_{1})}G_{T_{1}}(r)^{N-1}F(r)^{k}(1-F(r))dr,\\
&amp;\!\!\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\text{if
}t=1,\ldots,T_{1},u_{T_{1}+k}(T_{1})&gt;u_{t}(T_{1})&gt;u_{T_{1}+k+1}(T_{1}),\\
&amp;\!\!\;\;\;\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad k=1,\ldots,T-T_{1}-1,\\
\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(23)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle u_{t}(T_{1})-\mu=(T-t)\bigg(\int^{1}_{u_{t}(T_{1})}1-F(r)dr\bigg),\;\text{if }t=T_{1}+1,\ldots,T," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mstyle><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{t}(T_{1})-\mu=(T-t)\bigg(\int^{1}_{u_{t}(T_{1})}1-F(r)dr\bigg),\;\text{if }t=T_{1}+1,\ldots,T,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{T_{1}}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{T_{1}}(r)</annotation></semantics></math> is given in equation (<a href="#S4.EGx16" title="Lemma 4.2. ‣ 4.1. Analysis of Existing Centralized Communications Policy ‣ 4. Dcentralized Communication Mechanism for Non-myopic Agents ‣ Human-in-the-loop Learning Through Decentralized Communication Mechanisms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) by substituting <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-1</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and replacing <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(T_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(T_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. The resultant social welfare over the entire time horizon is</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
